WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.351
A printer can follow a perfect toolpath while the deposited

00:00:04.351 --> 00:00:08.151
material collapses. The rheology of the ink sets the rules.

00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:16.319
The printing material must move through a nozzle, then

00:00:16.319 --> 00:00:20.420
retain enough structure after deposition to realise the intended form.

00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:29.737
The forthcoming chapter considers three-, four-, five- and

00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:34.758
six-dimensional food-printing concepts, including responsive structures and programmed behaviour.

00:00:39.758 --> 00:00:44.690
Composition, texture and printing conditions interact. A promising

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.621
design requires material-specific testing before its properties are asserted.

00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:59.566
Time-responsive and higher-dimensional labels describe proposed capabilities; they do

00:00:59.566 --> 00:01:04.624
not establish that every structure has been practically demonstrated.

00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:14.643
This accepted chapter explores the design constraints for programmable

00:01:14.643 --> 00:01:19.611
edible materials. It remains forthcoming, not a completed laboratory result.
